\f1\fs22 She woke at the edge of a nightmare\'97a moment that, as it happened, was mid-afternoon. No judgments on the nana nap, even before motherhood or true old age. Exhaustion, both physical and psychic (recurring nightmares, day-terror reruns, and the occasional accidental bathtub drowning), had hollowed her out.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Boredom was a factor\'97though \'93trapped\'94 described it better. Perhaps her unstimulated brain was keeping itself fit with internal horror films.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The pattern repeated itself: Sarah bolt upright, throat raw, terror filling her lungs. She groped for her nightstand glass, drank until she nearly drowned, and saw Kathy appear in the doorway just as she began hacking.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Another bad dream, Holy Momma?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah grimaced. \'93Will you please stop calling me that.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy just smiled, deft as always at dissipating nightmare residue.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Pregnancy really suits you. You\'92re positively radiant.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah grunted. \'93Ha ha. You look fantastic too,\'94 she said, then caught herself\'97if anyone was glowing these days, it was Kathy. Vivacious, sharper, brighter every week. \'93What\'92s your secret?\'94 she asked before she could stop herself. Given how entangled they were, Sarah worried Kathy\'92s vitality might have the same source.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy perched on the bed, traced Sarah\'92s cheek. \'93Having someone to care for.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah bristled, unsure if it was lust or dependency\'97or both. \'93Not like you need to look after me.\'94 She stalled: who would want her now? She was just a vehicle for Frankenstein genes.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A hot tremor ran through her belly. Kathy noticed instantly. \'93It\'92s still too early for Little Clinton to be kicking.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I know,\'94 Sarah said, softly. But this wasn\'92t flutters. It felt more like a broadcast.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy retrieved the sphygmomanometer from the bedside table.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Blood pressure again?\'94 Sarah sighed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Just hold still, okay?\'94 Kathy was trying to sound calm; nobody wanted a patched-up foetus.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah offered her arm and endured the automatic squeeze of the cuff. Kathy hid the reading before Sarah could see.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How is it?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93High.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How high?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy\'92s frown deepened. \'93High.\'94 She tucked it away and sat beside her again. \'93Same nightmare?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah shrugged. \'93Not quite. Still trapped, looping, can\'92t breathe or push out the panic. There\'92s that smell, like someone boiled a dozen lab coats in fish stock.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy grimaced.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Well, something like that, anyway. But there was a pattern this time. Only a flash\'97I don\'92t think I dreamed it so much as\'85 woke up with it etched behind my eyes, like after you stare at something too bright. Just hanging there. But then it was moving, unfolding. I couldn\'92t focus on it.\'94 Sarah shivered.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy set a hesitant hand on Sarah\'92s thigh.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah gulped and shook; she fought back tears, then blurted. \'93I\'92m terrified of it. I know that sounds stupid, but there\'92s something\'85 
\f2\i evil
\f1\i0  is not the right word, but it feels so old. And indifferent.  It doesn\'92t care about us.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Us?\'94 Kathy was never down for any kind of dependency. Yet. 
\f2\i Damn
\f1\i0 . Kathy\'92s fingernails scraped at the bedsheet.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Us. All of us, this world\'85 perhaps the whole universe.\'94 Sarah looked like she\'92d met her own ghost. She grabbed Kathy\'92s shoulder, as if to steady herself. She gulped to fight back nausea. \'93I have the weirdest sense I\'92ve seen it before. It\'92s pulling me and I can\'92t get away. And\'85\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93And what?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah pressed her palms to her knees, steadying herself, letting an ancient, heavy silence settle. \'93I can\'92t shake the feeling that someone is gossiping about my haircut.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Kathy tried to smile. \'93You\'92re exhausted, that\'92s all. Try to rest. Want something to eat?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah shook her head. \'93Not yet. Maybe soon.\'94
